# East West Marquise Diamond Ring ({{year}}): 3 Styling Mistakes Cost $1,500

![Three loose marquise cut diamonds held in jeweller tweezers against a soft grey backdrop as the DiamondCritics featured image for the {{year}} east west marquise diamond ring guide titled 3 styling mistakes cost one thousand five hundred dollars](/images/east-west-marquise-diamond-ring.jpg "East West Marquise Diamond Ring 3 Styling Mistakes Cost 1500")

## TL;DR — East West Marquise Diamond Ring in One Scroll

**An east west marquise diamond ring sets the stone horizontally across the finger instead of lengthwise up the finger.** The elongated shape points side-to-side, not top-to-bottom. The visual effect: a dramatic sweep across the finger, a modern alternative to the classic vertical marquise, and a cleaner stack with wedding bands. Get the styling right and the ring reads uniquely elegant. Get it wrong and the $1,500 setting investment reads awkward or disproportionate.

**The 3 styling mistakes that cost $1,500 on an east west marquise (all 3 covered in full below):**

- **Mistake 1 — Picking east west on a short or wide finger.** East west maximises horizontal line — perfect for long/narrow fingers that need visual width broken up. On a short/wide finger it adds width to width and makes the finger look squarer.
- **Mistake 2 — Pairing east west with a contour wedding band.** Contour bands are shaped to curve around a vertical centre stone. On a horizontal east west, the contour sits awkwardly. Straight bands pair cleanly — spec straight at order.
- **Mistake 3 — Sizing up to see "bigger" stone face-up.** East west already maximises face-up width — the stone shows its full 15mm sideways. Buying a larger centre to look bigger wastes money. East west + 1ct = 1.5ct face-up perception on a classic vertical mount.

→ Entry pick: **[East West Solitaire in 14k Rose Gold at $995](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/east-west-solitaire-in-14k-rose-gold-item-193756?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — market floor, modern horizontal solitaire.
